<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persephone</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Proserpina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Persephone was a Greek goddess, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a>, carried off by Hades to be queen of the underworld. Her yearly return to her mother was linked to the growth of crops, and she was worshipped with Demeter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusinian-mysteries">Eleusinian Mysteries</a>. Locri Epizephyrii in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had a celebrated sanctuary of Persephone, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew her as Proserpina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persephone.jpg" alt="Persephone" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terracotta_votive_plaque_from_Eleusis,_450_BC,_NAMA,_190875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
